1
00:00:02,295 --> 00:00:05,536
So let's reiterate how we can store data in

2
00:00:05,633 --> 00:00:06,515
Deno apps.

3
00:00:07,157 --> 00:00:09,308
In our app, we have a lot of data typically,

4
00:00:09,741 --> 00:00:11,234
and we got a couple of different

5
00:00:11,571 --> 00:00:13,994
alternatives when it comes to how we wanna

6
00:00:13,994 --> 00:00:15,198
save that data.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,600
Now, one alternative which we already are

8
00:00:18,793 --> 00:00:21,104
using in basically any Deno app we're

9
00:00:21,136 --> 00:00:23,383
building right from the start is that we

10
00:00:23,383 --> 00:00:27,009
store data in variables or constants of that

11
00:00:27,057 --> 00:00:27,746
application.

12
00:00:28,596 --> 00:00:30,728
And this means that the data gets stored in

13
00:00:30,744 --> 00:00:33,213
memory and that's really important because

14
00:00:33,405 --> 00:00:35,794
the JavaScript engine and Deno in the end

15
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:38,984
therefore simply stores all the data you're

16
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:41,548
working with in your program in the memory

17
00:00:41,725 --> 00:00:44,274
that's allocated to that program. The

18
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:45,235
advantage of this

19
00:00:45,812 --> 00:00:48,441
is that this is super quick to access and

20
00:00:48,618 --> 00:00:50,125
offers great performance.

21
00:00:50,862 --> 00:00:53,731
The memory of your application or the memory

22
00:00:53,988 --> 00:00:57,146
on your computer in general is super fast to

23
00:00:57,274 --> 00:01:00,111
access and it's highly optimized for quick

24
00:01:00,159 --> 00:01:02,580
access and for giving you great performance.

25
00:01:03,205 --> 00:01:05,546
That's why the JavaScript engine does use

26
00:01:05,546 --> 00:01:08,110
the memory for storing variables and not

27
00:01:08,110 --> 00:01:09,762
temporary files, for example.

28
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:13,801
Now, the disadvantage, of course, is that

29
00:01:13,961 --> 00:01:16,959
this data doesn't persist. It's lost after

30
00:01:17,071 --> 00:01:19,700
every restart. Therefore, variables and

31
00:01:19,732 --> 00:01:21,720
constants are, of course, what you typically

32
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,429
use for all those results, temporary

33
00:01:24,525 --> 00:01:27,266
results, and intermediate results that you

34
00:01:27,266 --> 00:01:30,024
derive in your application, for all the data

35
00:01:29,992 --> 00:01:31,113
currently working with.

36
00:01:31,674 --> 00:01:33,822
But any data that should last a bit longer,

37
00:01:34,078 --> 00:01:36,722
for example, the products of an online shop

38
00:01:36,786 --> 00:01:39,831
or user data, such data should be stored in

39
00:01:39,831 --> 00:01:41,049
a more persistent way.

40
00:01:41,834 --> 00:01:43,757
And one way of storing data more

41
00:01:43,773 --> 00:01:46,866
persistently is to store it in files. So

42
00:01:46,962 --> 00:01:48,356
using the file system,

43
00:01:48,965 --> 00:01:51,706
we can create a new file and write data into

44
00:01:51,706 --> 00:01:53,949
it. And of course, we can also read data

45
00:01:54,029 --> 00:01:54,622
from a file.

46
00:01:55,472 --> 00:01:57,795
So storing data in files is not too bad, but

47
00:01:57,955 --> 00:02:01,096
it has one big disadvantage. It's relatively

48
00:02:01,096 --> 00:02:04,851
slow. Offers bad performance. Now of course

49
00:02:05,316 --> 00:02:07,098
it really comes down to the exact use case

50
00:02:07,098 --> 00:02:09,297
and what you're writing and reading, but in

51
00:02:09,425 --> 00:02:11,961
general you don't want to write data to

52
00:02:12,057 --> 00:02:14,673
files which you then need to use right away.

53
00:02:14,962 --> 00:02:17,418
Or if you need to read in a lot of data, you

54
00:02:17,530 --> 00:02:19,729
might not want to do that with files alone.

55
00:02:20,724 --> 00:02:22,201
The advantage of course is that the data

56
00:02:22,345 --> 00:02:25,363
does persist though, so it survives restart

57
00:02:25,459 --> 00:02:27,930
of your Deno app. So that's a good thing to

58
00:02:27,946 --> 00:02:28,171
have.

59
00:02:29,085 --> 00:02:31,618
But because files are relatively slow to

60
00:02:31,714 --> 00:02:34,487
access, we typically don't use them for

61
00:02:34,616 --> 00:02:36,972
permanent storage, or at least not alone.

62
00:02:37,501 --> 00:02:40,563
Instead, we use databases. And there we got

63
00:02:40,563 --> 00:02:42,519
a couple of different database engines,

64
00:02:42,808 --> 00:02:44,635
different database systems we can use.

65
00:02:45,196 --> 00:02:47,537
Now, under the hood, databases also work

66
00:02:47,601 --> 00:02:50,567
with files, but they add an extra layer on

67
00:02:50,567 --> 00:02:53,020
top of those files where they basically can

68
00:02:53,052 --> 00:02:55,665
load data into memory, cache it, and

69
00:02:55,697 --> 00:02:58,070
therefore make it faster for you to query

70
00:02:58,182 --> 00:03:00,635
that data and to work with that data. So

71
00:03:00,651 --> 00:03:02,959
there's simply an extra layer on top of

72
00:03:03,152 --> 00:03:06,085
files, making it faster to work with data in

73
00:03:06,101 --> 00:03:08,570
general. And therefore you have relatively

74
00:03:08,586 --> 00:03:11,375
fast data access when working with databases

75
00:03:11,712 --> 00:03:12,818
and a good performance.

76
00:03:13,427 --> 00:03:15,190
Certainly not the speed you have with

77
00:03:15,238 --> 00:03:17,354
variables, but you'll have all the speed and

78
00:03:17,354 --> 00:03:20,047
performance you need in applications to work

79
00:03:20,047 --> 00:03:21,153
with data efficiently.

80
00:03:22,051 --> 00:03:24,552
And of course, the big advantage, just like

81
00:03:24,552 --> 00:03:28,383
with files, is that data does persist. So it

82
00:03:28,463 --> 00:03:31,045
also survives the restart. Of your Deno app.

83
00:03:31,767 --> 00:03:33,179
So these are your different storage

84
00:03:33,532 --> 00:03:36,098
mechanisms. All of them matter but for

85
00:03:36,163 --> 00:03:38,200
different purposes. And now we're going to

86
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,136
have a look at database storage, which is a

87
00:03:41,136 --> 00:03:43,847
super important way of storing data in any

88
00:03:43,943 --> 00:03:44,730
app you're building.

89
00:03:45,548 --> 00:03:47,216
Now when it comes to storing data with

90
00:03:47,248 --> 00:03:50,024
databases, you don't just have one database

91
00:03:50,136 --> 00:03:51,869
that you can use. There are tons of

92
00:03:51,965 --> 00:03:53,986
different database engines based on

93
00:03:53,986 --> 00:03:56,922
